  <iati-identifier>CH-FDJP-CHE296622304-Cprofiles</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="CH-FDJP-CHE296622304" secondary-reporter="0" type="15">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Global Initiative Against Transnational Organized Crime</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Characterization of Latin American Countries&#x2019; Criminal Ecosystems</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The primary target groups include UK Government departments and agencies engaged in the Serious and Organised Crime Joint Analysis (SOCJA) process, as well as policymakers, researchers, and international partners working on security, governance, and development issues in Latin America.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project seeks to: 
&#x2022; Develop 15 country profiles detailing the main characteristics of organized crime in Latin AmericaN
&#x2022; Produce three analytical briefs focused on (i) regional organized crime trends, (ii) environmental crime in the Amazon, and (iii) illicit financial flows in Brazil.
These deliverables aim to support the UK Government&#x2019;s threat assessments and policy responses by providing actionable evidence based on data analysis, literature review, and stakeholder engagement.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project, implemented by the Global Initiative Against Transnational Organized Crime (GI-TOC), aims to strengthen understanding of organized crime dynamics across Latin America through the development of fifteen country profiles and three regional analytical briefs. The initiative is part of the UK&#x2019;s Serious and Organised Crime (SOC) analytical framework and provides strategic insights to inform programming and policy priorities in the region.</narrative>
  </description>
